Between a quarter and a half of the illustrative vocabulary is shared, and
pretending otherwise would be silly. These are the standard illustrations for
these rules, for several rules they are close to the only ones that exist, and
we learned the rules from that document.

What is NOT shared is any sequence of words that is not a citation or an
artefact of counting. Of the seventeen two-word matches listed above, thirteen
name an author, a document or a licence: "Ursula Pinheiro Weber", "Werner
Marti Bärndütschi Schrybwys", "CC BY". Three are not word pairs at all. They
are single letters that both texts name AS letters, which the word-splitter
then glues into a pair once it drops the punctuation between them: "b d" comes
from "a soft `b`, `d` or `g`", "n i" from "Wo-n-i", "u l" from "u + l". The
seventeenth is "wi ne", the linking-n construction the rule teaches rather
than an example anyone chose.

The earlier version of this paragraph claimed all sixteen others were
citations and named "wi ne" as the single exception, which the three letter
pairs quietly contradicted thirty lines further up in this same file. That is
the fourth time a hand-written sentence here has disagreed with the
measurement printed above it.

Getting there took work, and every step of it was found by measurement rather
than by re-reading. Five example phrases once stood verbatim in both ("wi ne
Bär", the three spellings of "Wi ni gseit ha", "mir luege aa") and are
replaced. Several example lists carried the PDF's ordering, which surfaced as
shared sequences ("lila d klinik", "stei spile starch", "umespringe
verstecke") and are reordered. One word, "schryne", was our typo for "schyne".

What is ours is the prose and the arrangement. The rulebook opens with a
section that document does not have ("What gives a text away"), and the
consonant section is ordered by what each phenomenon is rather than by the
PDF's numbered sequence. Neither document is redistributed with this
repository.

If Ursula Pinheiro-Weber, Hans Jürg Zingg or berndeutsch.ch consider any part
of rules/ to encroach on their work, open an issue and it will be rewritten or
removed. That is a standing offer, not a formality.

The grammar section
-------------------
The description of Bernese grammar (absence of a preterite, the double perfect,
the frequency of the subjunctive, the declension of zwe/zwo/zwöi and drei/drü,
politeness as the second person plural) follows the Alemannic Wikipedia article
"Berndeutsch", licensed CC BY-SA 4.0:

  https://als.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berndeutsch
  Authors: see the article's history page.

corpus/
-------
corpus/sources.md is ours. corpus/texts/ is not committed to this repository;
it is produced locally by scripts/bd-corpus, which downloads Alemannic
Wikipedia articles licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 and writes the attribution into the
header of every file it creates. If you redistribute those texts, CC BY-SA 4.0
applies to them, not MIT.

berndeutsch.ch
--------------
scripts/bdw queries https://www.berndeutsch.ch, a free Bernese-German
dictionary online since 2000 and run as 100% volunteer work by Stephan Burkard.
The site has no API. bdw therefore behaves like a courteous human reader: one
request per page, a delay between requests, a bounded page walk, a User-Agent
that names this repository, and a link back to every entry instead of a copy of
the dictionary. No dictionary content is stored in this repository.

If you find this useful, the dictionary is the reason. Add the words it is
missing: https://www.berndeutsch.ch/pages/wordaddinfo
